diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the outage. End-to-end reachability between racks is healthy: pc_0_0 can ping pc_0_1 and vice versa with 0% packet loss and ~1 ms RTT. The routing control plane is also up. On the leaves, BGP has installed the remote rack’s subnet via the spines (leaf_router_0_0 shows 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.4; leaf_router_0_1 shows 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6). All BGP adjacencies across the fabric (leaves, spines, super-spine) are Established and each side is exchanging the expected prefixes. This confirms the forwarding path leaf→spine→super-spine (and back) is operational.

Mechanism: If any middlebox or BGP session were down after maintenance, the leaves would lack routes to the opposite rack’s /24, causing ICMP to time out. Instead, routes are present and traffic passes, so the earlier symptom was transient and has cleared.

Localization: No device currently exhibits interface down, missing routes, or BGP faults. All relevant processes (FRR/BGP) and links appear normal across leaf_router_0_0, leaf_router_0_1, spine_router_0_0, spine_router_0_1, and super_spine_router_0.

Root cause: The outage is not present now; most likely a temporary control-plane disruption during the maintenance window that has since recovered.

Corrective fix: No immediate changes required. Recommend adding a post-maintenance verification step (fabric-wide ping and BGP neighbor checks) and enabling synthetic reachability monitoring/alerts so any future transient issues are detected and confirmed automatically. If the problem recurs, capture timestamps to correlate with change events and logs. 
